.TH SCOPELINK-SIMULATOR 1 "2026-08-12" "indi-scopelink 1.0" "INDI Drivers"
.SH NAME
scopelink-simulator \- serve a simulated ScopeLink controller on a pseudo terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scopelink-simulator
.RB [ \-\-generation
.IR 2 | 3 | 4 ]
.RB [ \-\-interface\-minor
.IR 0 | 1 ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scopelink-simulator
answers the ScopeLink wire protocol on a pseudo terminal, so that anything which expects a serial port
can be exercised without a controller attached. It prints the path of the pseudo terminal it created and
then serves one client until it is stopped.
.PP
Use it when the thing being tested is a port:
.BR scopelink-cli (1),
a client's port handling, or the driver's own serial path. To try the
.B driver
without hardware there is no need for this program at all \- turn the driver's
.B SIMULATION
switch on instead, which connects it to the same simulated controller directly. See
.BR indi_scopelink (1).
.PP
It is not a model of the firmware. It answers every command the driver sends, with frame layouts taken
from the same measurements the driver's parsers are built on, and it moves its motors at a plausible
rate so that motion handling has something to follow. The values it reports are plausible rather than
measured.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-generation " 2|3|4"
Hardware generation to imitate. Defaults to 3. The generations differ in what they offer, not only in
what they report: generation 2 has no USB hub and no smart switch diagnostics, generation 3 has a two
port hub, and generation 4 has three motors and a six port hub.
.TP
.BI \-\-interface\-minor " 0|1"
Diagnostic interface version to report, defaulting to 1.0 \- or to 1.1 on generation 4, which has no 1.0
firmware. This is a separate axis from the generation, and it is the one that decides the frame layout:
1.1 drops the four digital inputs out of the middle of both frames and puts the state of the front flap
as a whole on the end of the status frame. The same generation 3 board runs either, so a status frame is
60 bytes on 1.0 and 57 on 1.1. Asking for generation 4 on 1.0 is refused rather than served.
.IP
1.1 is also where the motors stop being wired to their jobs. A simulated 1.1 controller holds the motor
assignment, applies the same rules to it that the firmware does, and answers the function commands that
drive the focuser, the rotator and the front flap by name rather than by motor. A 1.0 one holds no
assignment and refuses those commands, which is what makes the pair worth having.
.IP
It also takes that assignment when it starts, the way real firmware does: one written into it while it
runs is stored and reads straight back, and every command still goes where the assignment it started with
sends it. The reset command is what makes it read the configuration again, and that is what the driver's
.B Restart controller
action sends. Nothing else is reset, so the motors keep their positions.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
.B scopelink-simulator --generation 3
ScopeLink simulator, hardware generation 3 on interface 1.0
Port: /dev/pts/7

.B scopelink-cli --port /dev/pts/7 info

.B scopelink-simulator --generation 3 --interface-minor 1
ScopeLink simulator, hardware generation 3 on interface 1.1
Port: /dev/pts/8

.B scopelink-simulator --generation 4
ScopeLink simulator, hardware generation 4 on interface 1.1
Port: /dev/pts/9
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR indi_scopelink (1),
.BR scopelink-cli (1)
